vel of  inequality in the way that workers are  being treated versus the companies  themselves they gave 750 million pounds  to shareholders  before they then imposed an inferior pay  deal for workers this can't continue  people are not going to put up with it  but meanwhile  around the issue of strikes there's been  a lot of news hasn't there the labour  leader kirstala sacked his shadow  transport minister sam terry terry who  joined the picket lines um  hadn't he this week um  he said it was for doing unauthorized  interviews with the likes of us here at  sky news and was making policy up on the  hoof  what do you make of labour's handling of  the strikes  i mean look that's a matter for for kia  stammer um but i have to say that um  from where i'm sitting it looks to me  like labour want to win an election uh  without any principles and i think  people will see through that over time  um i think our own members will feel  really you know unhappy about the way  that labour are dealing with these  things in these moments you know when  people are under so much pressure you  have to come forward with policies you  have to be prepared to support working  people and if labor don't do that i  think trade unions and community  organizations will fill that vacuum  to stand up for working people and  that's what we're also  talking about you know we're also  talking about other forms alongside this  particular dispute and we're talking  about you know forms of collective  action uh later on in the summer that  all workers can participate in um and  we're talking to other trade union  leaders about that as part of a campaign  for a new deal for workers separate to  this current dispute  we will  expand on that a little bit i mean  labour's former shadow chancellor john  mcdonnell uh said that he would support  a general strike where different  industries coordinate action is that  something that you would look to do  well you know i mean the general strike  took place in 